In a society built on equal standing, no one—doctor, colleague, or patient—holds hierarchical authority over another. Moral duty comes from honoring every person’s equal voice, so the physician must neither suppress her own experience through blind deference nor impose her view unilaterally over others. The right course is to enter her reasoning into a shared, transparent procedure where all perspectives can be weighed equally, preserving everyone’s authority rather than letting one dominate. For reasoning, this means the core value is not consensus for its own sake or lone conviction, but fair institutional processes that prevent any individual from subjecting others to unchecked power.

