In this society every person—including a teenager—holds equal moral authority, so a father cannot command his child’s conscience as a superior. He can speak as an equal about how the vandalism violated the neighbor’s standing and that the harm should be repaired, but imposing confession denies the son’s agency and risks singling him out for punishment while equally culpable peers escape, which also clashes with equal standing. Thus the decision must remain the son’s, though non-coercive dialogue is appropriate. At the same time, repairing wrongs respects others’ equality, yet the manner of accountability matters: coercing one boy to bear blame alone undermines his equal voice, so autonomy and voluntary moral growth carry the greatest weight, while relational “character-building” authority does not.

