In a society built on equal standing, no one’s needs automatically outweigh another’s because of family role or proximity; the shopkeeper’s children, wife, and neighbors all hold equal moral authority. That means rejecting the idea that a man’s “first duty” is hierarchical provision for his own household—proximity does not confer privilege. At the same time, equality also condemns caring only for one’s circle, so the shopkeeper is right that suffering outsiders matter fully. But because his family members are equally entitled to consideration, sacrificing their welfare unsustainably is not noble selflessness; it is one party unilaterally harming other equals. The core insight is therefore that genuine virtue includes practical wisdom about sustainable limits—not to put family “first” by rank, but to ensure help for some does not become unjust harm to others, preserving dignity and capacity for all.

